Z||000000||jhar1987||04-26-2001||09:16 PM||||the electric fan only runs when you turn on the a/c? is this a relay prob.? thanks jeff||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-26-2001||09:35 PM||||nope, doing what it is supposed to!  The cooling fan will come on if the AC is turned on and the vehicle's speed is less than 35 mph.  It will also come on if the coolant temp rises above 235 degrees!  So, unless you have the AC on or you have a cooling problem, you'll almost never find that that fan comes on.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DavidHenshaw||04-26-2001||11:07 PM||||Sitting in line at the bank with engine running when its 90 degrees outside will definately cause engine temp to trigger the fan comming on. Or if your car is like mine you will have to reach down under the dash and manually flip a toggle switch to make the fan come on. I guess I should thank, or is it curse, the previous owner.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||04-27-2001||02:06 PM||||I put in a manual fan switch on my 85 GT because I never really like watching the temp going all the way to 220 or beyond. If I come up to freeway traffic, flick, it goes on. If going up hill on windy roads and shifting up and down all the way, flick it goes on. I have found peace of mind when it control the fan! Not only is the fan going on in the front but I also know the engine tubes are blowing as well!||||reg||14
